\fs96 \cf2 Generics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Generics let you write code that builds and runs across many types. This matters because you don't have to write type-specific versions of the same logic over and over again. Instead of branching on "this type" versus "that one," you can collapse nearly identical implementations into a single, maintainable solution.\
You don't duplicate work. You build unified solutions that automatically adapt at compile time to the types you already use and the types you'll use in the future. It doesn't matter whether those types come from your own code or from libraries you didn't write.\
Generics work through traits to make this possible. Traits define what a type must be able to do, and generics let you write code that operates over any type that meets those trait requirements. One type, method, or function can then work across many source types, eliminating redundant "almost the same" implementations and centralizing fixes in one place.\
At their core, generics give you the power to make code work with any type that fits its defined requirements. You express your intent once and apply it broadly, without sacrificing correctness or clarity.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Generic code creates custom compiled code for each concrete type it's used with. While this enables performance enhancements and supports static checking, this will increase both compile times and code size.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Setting generic parameter bounds\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Bounds specify what a type must be able to do. They're requirements written as traits that restrict which types can be used with generic code.\
You must always restrict generic parameters with trait bounds. Traits define the vocabulary generic code relies on. They specify available operations, guarantees, and associated types (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 comptime
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  aliases for related types). This vocabulary allows generic code to express behavior, access related types, and gives the compiler enough information to reason about correctness. This lets it emit warnings about missing elements, whether those elements relate to type capabilities or lifetime management.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
With generics, the most permissive trait bound is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/AnyType/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \ul \ulc5 AnyType}}. It places no behavioral requirements on a type and serves as the least restrictive bound available.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/builtin/anytype/ImplicitlyDestructible/"}}{\fldrslt \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\ul ImplicitlyDestructible}}
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 is another baseline trait. It is the base trait for types that require lifetime management using destructors. Any type that needs cleanup when it goes out of scope should implement this trait. Generic code that stores or owns values often relies on it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Bounds are what make generic code workable. Without them, the compiler can't allow useful operations, and functions and methods can't do anything meaningful.\
Examples throughout this page use trait composition (like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Equatable & Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to spell out the features their algorithms depend on. Each trait defines required API elements that types used from call sites must provide.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Basic generics: compare two lists\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Consider comparing two lists to test if they contain the same values in the same order.\
You could write a separate implementation for each element type used in lists. Or you could write a single generic function that works for any list whose elements can be compared.\
Here's a concrete version that's only useful for Integers:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  \cf16 all_equal_int\cf5 (ref\cf15  lhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 ref\cf15  rhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ])\cf15  \cf5 ->\cf15  Bool\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf15  \cf16 len\cf5 (\cf15 lhs\cf5 )\cf15  \cf5 !=\cf15  \cf16 len\cf5 (\cf15 rhs\cf5 ):\cf15  \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 False\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 for\cf15  left\cf5 ,\cf15  right \cf5 in\cf15  \cf16 zip\cf5 (\cf15 lhs\cf5 ,\cf15  rhs\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 if\cf15  left \cf5 !=\cf15  right\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 False\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 True\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Unlike this function, a generic version doesn't care which element type it uses. It only demands the capabilities the algorithm uses: elements must be comparable for equality. They're used as loop values, so they must be copyable.\
Given those requirements, your function can walk both lists element-by-element, just like the Integer version.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  all_equal\cf5 [\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     T\cf5 :\cf15  Equatable \cf5 &\cf15  Copyable\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 ](ref\cf15  lhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 ref\cf15  rhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 ])\cf15  \cf5 ->\cf15  Bool\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 if\cf15  \cf16 len\cf5 (\cf15 lhs\cf5 )\cf15  \cf5 !=\cf15  \cf16 len\cf5 (\cf15 rhs\cf5 ):\cf15  \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 False\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 for\cf15  left\cf5 ,\cf15  right \cf5 in\cf15  \cf16 zip\cf5 (\cf15 lhs\cf5 ,\cf15  rhs\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 if\cf15  left \cf5 !=\cf15  right\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14             \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 False\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 return\cf15  \cf17 True\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Both implementations check the list lengths, returning 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  if they're not the same. Then they walk the list, using an early return of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 False
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  on the first mismatch. If the function makes it to the end, it returns 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . No differences were found.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 How this works\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The generic parts in this example make the code reusable across many types. The first sign that it's generic is the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type parameter. You declare type parameters in square brackets before the function arguments. Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  represents the element type used by both lists.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 Mojo favors names that show purpose over type. It adopts naming conventions shared across modern programming languages, including Rust and C++, which helps keep generic signatures and parameter names readable.\cb1 \
\cb12 Mojo type parameter names use PascalCase. They may be short (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 T
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 E
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 ) or descriptive (
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ErrorType
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Element
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Count
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 ). Common conventions include:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
T
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 U
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 V
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  general type parameters\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
K
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 V
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  key and value types\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
H
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  hasher types or functions\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
E
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  error types, as in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Result<T, E>
\fs28 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When you call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 all_equal()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the compiler looks at the call site to determine 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 's type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 print(\cf21 "Int (Expect True):\\t"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     all_equal\cf5 ([\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 3\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 [\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 3\cf5 ]))\cf15  \cf6 # True\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf21 "Int (Expect False):\\t"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     all_equal\cf5 ([\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 2\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 3\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 [\cf17 4\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 5\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf17 6\cf5 ]))\cf15  \cf6 # False\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf21 "String (Expect True):\\t"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     all_equal\cf5 ([\cf21 "hello"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf21 "world"\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 [\cf21 "hello"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf21 "world"\cf5 ]))\cf15  \cf6 # True\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf21 "String (Expect False):\\t"\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     all_equal\cf5 ([\cf21 "hello"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf21 "world"\cf5 ],\cf15  \cf5 [\cf21 "goodbye"\cf5 ,\cf15  \cf21 "world"\cf5 ]))\cf15  \cf6 # False\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The compiler generates a concrete function, a custom, type-specific version of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 all_equal()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for each type used. In this example, it creates two versions, one for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and one for 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 In type theory terms, the parametric 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 all_equal()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  function is polymorphic. The concrete, type-specific versions are monomorphic.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Type requirements\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Equatable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is required by the example algorithm. 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is often needed for loops and container elements. These are compiler-enforced, so you won't have to guess which ones you need.\
Keep your requirements as minimal as your code allows:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 T\cf5 :\cf15  Equatable \cf5 &\cf15  Copyable\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The & symbol you see here forms your trait composition. It means: "both of these requirements apply."\
Always use the fewest bounds you can get away with. This offers your code the greatest flexibility and the widest type compatibility. For example, if you remove the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Equatable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait bound from 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 all_equal()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the code won't compile. Mojo can't guarantee that all possible values of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  implement support for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 !=
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  operator.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 A type provides operator support for 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 !=
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  by implementing the 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 __ne__()
\fs28 \cf2 \cb12  dunder function.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Mojo compiler reliably emits errors when your code calls a function or accesses a member that isn't enumerated by trait bounds. The error message shows that the generic type is underspecified with respect to behavior: the Mojo compiler can't assume the requested functionality exists for every type that satisfies the current bounds, so it reports that back to you.\
To fix this, you expand the generic type's trait bounds, increasing its vocabulary. You normally do this with trait composition, adding additional traits to the type parameter's bounds. Although these additional bounds are technically restrictions or constraints, they actually increase the API surface available to the generic function. Adding more traits exposes functionality that generic abstraction hides, allowing the compiler to reason more precisely about lifetimes, side effects, and which type members are visible.\
To conclude, generic errors occur when a type's trait bounds don't include the functionality the code relies on. Fixing them means you need to expand those bounds to make that behavior visible to the compiler.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb12 In type theory terms, bounds ensure your code is sound. This means that trait elements used by your implementation are guaranteed to exist for any type that satisfies the bounds.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Generic parameter types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In this example, both parameters are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 List
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 s that use the same element type:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf15 \cb14 lhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 ],\cf15  rhs\cf5 :\cf15  List\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 ]\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for both arguments ensures your loop compares like-with-like and prevents type mismatches.\
The previous example showed lists containing elements of type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Generic types can also be used without embedding them into container types, like you see here:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  my_generic_fn\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  AnyType\cf5 ](\cf15 value\cf5 :\cf15  T\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This function accepts any type, because its only limit is 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is the root of the trait hierarchy. It's the baseline that all types extend. Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 AnyType
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  means the value has no guaranteed destructor or lifetime management. Outside of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/reflection/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 reflection}} (type introspection), this function is effectively useless.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Generic types\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 So far, you've seen generics applied to methods and functions. Mojo also lets you define generic types. These use compile-time parameters to define both their fields and their methods.\
Generic types let you package a reusable shape: stored fields plus supported operations. Instead of coding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 PairInt
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 PairString
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and so on, you write one 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and let the compiler generate specialized versions for each concrete 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  you use.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf15  ComparableValue \cf5 =\cf15  Equatable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyCopyable\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Pair\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  ComparableValue\cf5 ](\cf15 ComparableValue\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  left\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  right\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __eq__\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 ,\cf15  other\cf5 :\cf15  Pair\cf5 [\cf15 Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cf5 ])\cf15  \cf5 ->\cf15  Bool\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf15  self\cf5 .\cf15 left \cf5 ==\cf15  other\cf5 .\cf15 left \cf5 and\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14                self\cf5 .\cf15 right \cf5 ==\cf15  other\cf5 .\cf15 right\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Like generic functions, generic types use placeholder parameters. The difference is that a generic type uses those parameters in its storage as well as in its methods. Here, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  stores two values of the same element type and implements equality by comparing its fields.\
Traits are what make generic types practical. Those bounds describe the requirements the type definition depends on. In this example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  needs to compare values, so 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must be equatable. It also needs to assign and copy values in common operations, so it applies a trait composition bound to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf15  ComparableValue \cf5 =\cf15  Equatable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyCopyable\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  definition applies this bound in two places: to the type itself (in parentheses) and to the type parameter (in square brackets), which is used to define its fields.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Pair\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  ComparableValue\cf5 ](\cf15 ComparableValue\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For the parentheses after the type, you can say the struct "conforms to" or "implements" this bound. This is a promise. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type is saying, "I am a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 ComparableValue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ."\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
For the square brackets, the trait bound restricts which values callers can pass. This is a requirement: "Any value used with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must have type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must be a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 ComparableValue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This makes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  usable anywhere a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 ComparableValue
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is required (because of the parentheses) and ensures all 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  values conform (because of the square brackets).\
So far, these examples use type parameters. Generic types can also take non-type parameters, which you'll see next.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Adding non-generic parameters\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Generic code doesn't keep you from using other parameters. Include whatever parameters you need to fully define the functionality you're building:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 ExampleStruct\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  example\cf5 [\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         T\cf5 :\cf15  Writable \cf5 &\cf15  Copyable\cf5 ,\cf15    \cf6 # generic type parameter\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         count\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cf5 ,\cf15                \cf6 # parameter\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 ](\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         self\cf5 ,\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         data\cf5 :\cf15  String\cf5 ,\cf15       \cf6 # function argument\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         init_value\cf5 :\cf15  T      \cf6 # generic argument\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 )\cf15  \cf5 ->\cf15  String\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 By convention, Mojo uses lower_snake_case for parameter names that define compile-time values. This helps distinguish them from type parameters.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Downcasting safely: 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb13 conforms_to
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  + 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb13 trait_downcast()
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Sometimes a generic parameter's bound is broader than what you actually need. You may require operations from a specific trait that a parameter's bound doesn't guarantee. In those cases, Mojo provides a guarded downcast pattern using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 conforms_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 trait_downcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
Downcasting is valid when the value's underlying type actually implements the target trait. Check for that conformance first. Once it's confirmed, you can safely downcast and treat the value as having that trait.\
This pattern keeps your code correct and makes fallback behavior explicit:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 def\cf15  process\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  AnyType\cf5 ](\cf15 value\cf5 :\cf15  T\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 comptime\cf15  \cf5 if\cf15  conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 T\cf5 ,\cf15  Writable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 var\cf15  w \cf5 =\cf15  trait_downcast\cf5 [\cf15 Writable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyCopyable\cf5 ](\cf15 value\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf15 w\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 else:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 print(\cf21 "<not writable>"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # Alternatively, you can fail at compile time using assertions\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf6 # with `comptime assert`\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Key points:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
conforms_to(T, Writable & ImplicitlyCopyable)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  checks whether type 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conforms to the given trait composition.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
trait_downcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  rebinds a value to a trait-bounded view at compile time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Only call 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 trait_downcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  inside the guarded branch. Use the else branch to handle non-conforming values. If a trait is always required, apply the bound directly, such as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T: Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , instead of using a guarded downcast.\
Limit 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 trait_downcast()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to cases where you can't express the requirement as a generic bound. This pattern most often appears in reflection code, where you need to handle values conditionally based on their traits. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/reflection/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 reflection}} for details.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 When generics get more complex\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The examples on this page focus on the most common and useful cases for working with generics. As you progress to larger APIs and more abstract code, you'll likely encounter a few more generic patterns. Here's a quick summary of the most likely ones you'll see:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Associated (dependent) types: Some traits declare {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/traits/#comptime-members-for-generics"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 associated types}}. For example, collection and iteration traits use an associated element type (commonly named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Element
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to specify the values the collection owns. Associated types can also constrain behavior. For instance, they can require that collection elements be hashable or implicitly destructible.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Generic methods in non-generic types: Types don't have to be generic themselves to use generics. Individual methods can introduce their own generic type parameters when only part of an API needs them. This is common when a type processes values of different types, but isn't tied to a single type at construction.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Multiple type parameters: Most generics use a single type parameter, but some algorithms work with two or more. In those cases, traits describe how the parameters relate to each other, not just what each type can do on its own. Common examples include dictionaries and hash tables (keys and values), caches and memoization (also keys and values), database relations (source and target types), operation results (values and errors), and zippers that operate across multiple types.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These topics build on the same core ideas shown here: type parameters, bounds, and specialization. Bounds can appear on functions, methods, types, or helper aliases using comptime, keeping capability requirements close to where they matter. You won't need these patterns for simple cases, but they become important as generic code grows.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Generics and explicit destruction\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Explicitly destroyed types sometimes don't work well with some generic code. The problem with this combination isn't generics, it's lifetime management.\
Explicit destruction exists so you can control teardown. With it, you can create destructors that take arguments, follow multiple destruction paths, or allow chaining and error-raising.\
Explicitly destroyed types provide a safe and flexible way to manage value lifetimes. Generic code that copies or moves values can't see or honor that logic. Once a value is copied or transferred, you've lost control of how (or whether) it's cleaned up, and your code won't compile.\
The danger zone includes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Generic code that manages lifetimes\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Generic code that copies or transfers values\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 These scenarios occur most often with generic containers, collections, and iterators, which copy or take ownership of their members and decide when transfer and destruction happen.\
The safe zone includes:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Generic operations that don't touch lifetimes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Think comparisons, predicates, and pure operations.\
As a working rule of thumb, if your generic code needs to own, copy, or decide when a value dies, avoid explicitly destroyed types. Add 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  bounds to bypass any issues.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Conditional trait conformance\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf22 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf22 \cb23 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb23 Conditional trait conformance is new and evolving. Certain capabilities may be unstable or unusable during roll-out. Use caution with 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Copyable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 Movable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 RegisterPassable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 , 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 TrivialRegisterPassable
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 , and 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb20 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs28 \cf2 \cb23 .\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Conditional trait conformance uses checks before allowing a type to adopt a trait. If the condition is satisfied, the type conforms. It must fulfill the trait's requirements by providing required methods and associated types, and it gains any default implementation provided by the trait. The type can then be used anywhere the trait is required, such as when passing it to a function that expects a conforming type.\
When the condition isn't satisfied, Mojo skips the conformance. You can't use the fully concrete type in contexts that require the trait.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Example: derived conformance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In the following declaration, Mojo conforms 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Wrapper
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when its parameter, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 T
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , is also 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf15  BaseTraits \cf5 =\cf15  Copyable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyDestructible\cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  BaseTraits\cf5 ](\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     Writable where conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 T\cf5 ,\cf15  Writable\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  value\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When conforming to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , Wrapper doesn't need to implement any methods to enable printing. The trait provides a default implementation of the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method.\
Now consider a type that isn't 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , that you might add into a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Wrapper
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 NotWritable\cf5 (\cf15 BaseTraits\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  data\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When instantiated with an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  (both 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Wrapper
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  gains 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conformance. With 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 NotWritable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , you can build a struct, but you can't print it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_int \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ](\cf17 42\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # Int is Writable\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 w_int\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # Wrapper[Int](value=42)\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_str \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 String\cf5 ](\cf21 "Hello"\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # String is Writable\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf15 w_str\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # Wrapper[String](value=Hello)\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # OK: only `Writable` conformance is unavailable\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_not_writable \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 NotWritable\cf5 ](\cf15 NotWritable\cf5 (\cf17 10\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # print(w_not_writable)  # Compile-time error:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb14 # invalid call to 'print': could not convert element of 'values' with\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb14 # type 'Wrapper[NotWritable]' to expected type 'Writable'\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Since 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 NotWritable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't conform to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the conditional conformance check fails and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Wrapper[NotWritable]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  doesn't adopt the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conformance. In contrast, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 String
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and their wrappers gain the conformance. This allows their instances to print by using the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 write_to()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method provided by 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Writable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
This pattern is standard for single-type containers like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Optional[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Box[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Lazy[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 List[T]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . It says: "This type can do X if its inner type can do X."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Example: parts conformance\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Conditional conformance has another standard pattern for types with multiple distinct components: 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Result[T, E]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair[L, R]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Dict[K, V]
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , and similar. It says: "This type can do X if each of its parts can do X":\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 comptime\cf15  BaseTraits \cf5 =\cf15  Copyable \cf5 &\cf15  ImplicitlyDestructible\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Pair\cf5 [\cf15 L\cf5 :\cf15  BaseTraits\cf5 ,\cf15  R\cf5 :\cf15  BaseTraits\cf5 ](\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     Hashable where conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 L\cf5 ,\cf15  Hashable\cf5 )\cf15  \cf5 and\cf15  conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 R\cf5 ,\cf15  Hashable\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  left\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 L\cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  right\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 R\cb1 \
\
\
\cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 NotHashable\cf5 (\cf15 BaseTraits\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  data\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  uses two distinct type parameters. When both are 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Hashable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the concrete 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Pair
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type becomes 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Hashable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , gaining the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 hash()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method from the trait:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  pair \cf5 =\cf15  Pair\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ,\cf15  String\cf5 ](\cf15 left\cf5 =\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf15  right\cf5 =\cf21 "one"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  \cf16 hash\cf15  \cf5 =\cf15  \cf16 hash\cf5 (\cf15 pair\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 print(\cf16 hash\cf5 )\cf15   \cf6 # Prints the hash of the pair\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # OK: only hashing is unavailable\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  pair2 \cf5 =\cf15  Pair\cf5 [\cf15 Int\cf5 ,\cf15  NotHashable\cf5 ](\cf15 left\cf5 =\cf17 1\cf5 ,\cf15  right\cf5 =\cf15 NotHashable\cf5 (\cf17 10\cf5 ))\cf15  \cf6 # Constructible\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # var hash2 = hash(pair2) # Compile time error\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Example: conditional method access\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When a type adopts a trait, it must satisfy the trait's required methods. The same conditions that determine whether a type can conditionally conform to a trait are usually the same conditions used to gate required method implementations.\
You express these method tests with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clauses. If true, the method is available. If not, the method can't be used for that specialized type. The following example walks you through this process and how it works.\
You can use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instances in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  statements. You make your 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Wrapper
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  testable by conforming to the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  trait. This trait requires a new method, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 __bool__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 T\cf5 :\cf15  BaseTraits\cf5 ](\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     Writable where conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 T\cf5 ,\cf15  Writable\cf5 ),\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     Boolable where conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 T\cf5 ,\cf15  Boolable\cf5 ),\cf15  \cf6 # New conditional conformance\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  value\cf5 :\cf15  Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cb1 \
\
\cb14     \cf6 # New method\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 def\cf15  \cf16 __bool__\cf5 (\cf15 self\cf5 )\cf15  \cf5 ->\cf15  Bool where conforms_to\cf5 (\cf15 Self\cf5 .\cf15 T\cf5 ,\cf15  Boolable\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14         \cf5 return\cf15  trait_downcast\cf5 [\cf15 Boolable\cf5 ](\cf15 self\cf5 .\cf15 value\cf5 ).\cf15 __bool__\cf5 ()\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, the condition on 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 __bool__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is the same as the one used for the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  conditional conformance. The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause gates the method. If the condition is true, the method is available. If not, it isn't.\
Since the method and the conformance use the same condition, they stay aligned. You won't end up in a situation where the wrapped type conforms but the method isn't available, or where the method exists without the corresponding conformance.\
You see this in the following examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_str \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 String\cf5 ](\cf21 "Hello"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 if\cf15  w_str\cf5 :\cf15   \cf6 # Chooses the non-empty branch\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "Non-empty string \\"\{w_str.value\}\\" is truthy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "Empty string \\"\{w_str.value\}\\" is falsy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_empty_str \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 String\cf5 ](\cf21 ""\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 if\cf15  w_empty_str\cf5 :\cf15   \cf6 # Chooses the empty branch\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "Non-empty string \\"\{w_empty_str.value\}\\" is truthy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "Empty string \\"\{w_empty_str.value\}\\" is falsy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 NotWritable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type from earlier in this section has no traits beyond the base 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Copyable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Therefore it isn't 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 Boolable
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and the condition on the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 __bool__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method will fail:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb14 @fieldwise_init\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 struct\cf15  \cf16 NotWritable\cf5 (\cf15 BaseTraits\cf5 ):\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 var\cf15  data\cf5 :\cf15  Int\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb14 var\cf15  w_not_writable \cf5 =\cf15  Wrapper\cf5 [\cf15 NotWritable\cf5 ](\cf15 NotWritable\cf5 (\cf17 10\cf5 ))\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb14 # The instance can't be used as a boolean value due to the\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb14 # the conditional method access for `__bool__()`.\cf15 \cb1 \
\
\cf6 \cb14 # Compiler error: the method condition is false\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb14 if\cf15  w_not_writable\cf5 :\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "NotWritable with data \{w_not_writable.value.data\} is truthy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb14 else:\cf15 \cb1 \
\cb14     \cf5 print(\cf15 t\cf21 "NotWritable with data \{w_not_writable.value.data\} is falsy"\cf5 )\cf15 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This condition on your method isn't conditional trait conformance. It's conditional method access. In this example, the method and the conformance use the same condition, so they stay aligned.\
That said, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb13 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clauses on methods and functions are useful even when you're not working with trait conformance. For example, you might gate a method so it only works with non-empty lists, real numbers, or with values that fall within the index bounds of a collection.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

